WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.160
There is a feeling every ambitious

00:00:02.320 --> 00:00:06.560
person knows. You read something

00:00:04.160 --> 00:00:08.640
important, it clicks, but then days

00:00:06.560 --> 00:00:11.320
later when you have to remember it under

00:00:08.640 --> 00:00:13.680
pressure, you go completely blank. For

00:00:11.320 --> 00:00:16.640
those who are new here, as CEO, board

00:00:13.680 --> 00:00:18.680
member, investor, I get to spend time in

00:00:16.640 --> 00:00:21.960
boardrooms of billion-dollar companies,

00:00:18.680 --> 00:00:24.440
and I see this pattern everywhere, over

00:00:21.960 --> 00:00:27.040
and over again. The people who keep

00:00:24.440 --> 00:00:29.600
pulling ahead are not the ones with the

00:00:27.040 --> 00:00:32.119
most information. They're the deep

00:00:29.600 --> 00:00:34.520
thinkers who can remember it, connect

00:00:32.119 --> 00:00:36.240
it, and use it better than everyone

00:00:34.520 --> 00:00:39.080
else. So, in this video, I want to show

00:00:36.240 --> 00:00:41.640
you how to learn fast and make it last

00:00:39.080 --> 00:00:43.800
in the AI era. A four-step system that

00:00:41.640 --> 00:00:46.200
helps you educate yourself like a

00:00:43.800 --> 00:00:48.520
genius. So, let's get started. The most

00:00:46.200 --> 00:00:50.640
dangerous solution in learning is this

00:00:48.520 --> 00:00:52.560
perception of instant clarity. I

00:00:50.640 --> 00:00:54.640
remember when I was working on Wall

00:00:52.560 --> 00:00:57.640
Street, I got on a call with one of the

00:00:54.640 --> 00:01:00.280
largest hedge fund clients, and I was

00:00:57.640 --> 00:01:03.000
[music] nervous. I had sent them a very

00:01:00.280 --> 00:01:05.360
dense spreadsheet with a model on what

00:01:03.000 --> 00:01:07.080
the future stock price could look like

00:01:05.360 --> 00:01:08.800
for a very large public company. [music]

00:01:07.080 --> 00:01:12.080
So, we were having a conversation about

00:01:08.800 --> 00:01:14.600
the model, and he suddenly asked me my

00:01:12.080 --> 00:01:17.280
opinion on a specific issue that was not

00:01:14.600 --> 00:01:19.600
about the model, and I remember going

00:01:17.280 --> 00:01:21.200
completely blank. An entire minute

00:01:19.600 --> 00:01:23.200
passed, [music] and I just completely

00:01:21.200 --> 00:01:25.440
froze. And then he said on the phone,

00:01:23.200 --> 00:01:28.560
"Hello, what's going on?" And I

00:01:25.440 --> 00:01:30.600
panicked, and I just hung up. Now, I had

00:01:28.560 --> 00:01:33.640
built the model, I had read all the

00:01:30.600 --> 00:01:35.920
data, I felt like I got it, but I hadn't

00:01:33.640 --> 00:01:37.800
mastered the story yet. I couldn't

00:01:35.920 --> 00:01:40.160
explain it to him. I had learned the

00:01:37.800 --> 00:01:42.680
musical notes, but I had not learned how

00:01:40.160 --> 00:01:44.920
to make music with them. That experience

00:01:42.680 --> 00:01:47.840
has a name [music] in cognitive science,

00:01:44.920 --> 00:01:50.920
the fluency illusion, followed by

00:01:47.840 --> 00:01:53.600
retrieval failure. When answers come

00:01:50.920 --> 00:01:55.840
easily and instantly, your brain signals

00:01:53.600 --> 00:01:58.800
that you have learned. You haven't.

00:01:55.840 --> 00:02:01.720
You've only recognized it. Recognizing

00:01:58.800 --> 00:02:04.400
and remembering are not cousins. They

00:02:01.720 --> 00:02:07.240
are completely different mental events.

00:02:04.400 --> 00:02:09.119
And AI has made this even worse. An

00:02:07.240 --> 00:02:11.560
instant answer feels like instant

00:02:09.119 --> 00:02:14.280
clarity. A polished explanation feels

00:02:11.560 --> 00:02:17.160
like mastery, but it's just an illusion.

00:02:14.280 --> 00:02:19.280
Borrowed fluency, no foundation. The

00:02:17.160 --> 00:02:21.240
smoother the experience, by the way, the

00:02:19.280 --> 00:02:23.840
more convincing the illusion. All the

00:02:21.240 --> 00:02:26.240
way back in the 1880s, Hermann

00:02:23.840 --> 00:02:29.560
Ebbinghaus ran memory experiments for

00:02:26.240 --> 00:02:32.480
years to understand why and how we

00:02:29.560 --> 00:02:36.360
forget. And he saw that forgetting

00:02:32.480 --> 00:02:39.160
follows a brutal curve. Roughly 70%

00:02:36.360 --> 00:02:41.320
of what we learn disappears within 24

00:02:39.160 --> 00:02:43.040
hours, tomorrow morning. But that

00:02:41.320 --> 00:02:44.959
doesn't mean your brain is broken,

00:02:43.040 --> 00:02:46.959
though. [music] It's doing exactly what

00:02:44.959 --> 00:02:49.880
it's supposed to do, what it's designed

00:02:46.959 --> 00:02:51.880
to do. Discard what is not repeated, so

00:02:49.880 --> 00:02:55.560
you can survive. Forgetting is not a

00:02:51.880 --> 00:02:57.520
flaw, it's the default feature. And that

00:02:55.560 --> 00:03:01.640
why you need a better framework, a

00:02:57.520 --> 00:03:04.680
system. I call it TRAP. Four moves that

00:03:01.640 --> 00:03:08.519
work with how memory is actually built.

00:03:04.680 --> 00:03:12.760
Test it, retain it, associate it,

00:03:08.519 --> 00:03:16.000
perform it. T R A P, TRAP. A page full

00:03:12.760 --> 00:03:19.040
of notes is not yet music. Notes are

00:03:16.000 --> 00:03:21.519
what you recognize. Music is what you

00:03:19.040 --> 00:03:23.920
play on your own. So, let's get to step

00:03:21.519 --> 00:03:26.239
one. This is how you break the fluency

00:03:23.920 --> 00:03:29.280
illusion. The first move in our

00:03:26.239 --> 00:03:32.760
framework, TRAP, is T for test. [music]

00:03:29.280 --> 00:03:35.040
Robert Bjork at UCLA spent decades

00:03:32.760 --> 00:03:37.760
studying what creates durable memory.

00:03:35.040 --> 00:03:41.360
His finding was deeply counterintuitive.

00:03:37.760 --> 00:03:45.320
When learning feels easy, very little

00:03:41.360 --> 00:03:47.600
durable memory is being built. Ease and

00:03:45.320 --> 00:03:49.160
retention often move in opposite

00:03:47.600 --> 00:03:51.217
directions. I thought that was

00:03:49.160 --> 00:03:51.239
fascinating. Bjork called these

00:03:51.217 --> 00:03:53.800
>> [music]

00:03:51.239 --> 00:03:57.000
>> desirable difficulties. The harder your

00:03:53.800 --> 00:03:59.120
brain has to work to pull the idea out,

00:03:57.000 --> 00:04:01.720
the stronger the memory becomes

00:03:59.120 --> 00:04:03.520
afterward. And a study published in

00:04:01.720 --> 00:04:05.760
Psychological Science tested [music]

00:04:03.520 --> 00:04:08.400
this directly. Two groups were given

00:04:05.760 --> 00:04:10.600
reading material. One group was tested

00:04:08.400 --> 00:04:13.040
on it, and the other was asked just to

00:04:10.600 --> 00:04:15.800
read it again. Same material, same time

00:04:13.040 --> 00:04:19.440
invested. One week later, the testing

00:04:15.800 --> 00:04:21.359
group retained 80% of it. The group that

00:04:19.440 --> 00:04:24.320
just read it again,

00:04:21.359 --> 00:04:27.160
34%. And that tells you that testing is

00:04:24.320 --> 00:04:29.280
not just about grades and scorecards. It

00:04:27.160 --> 00:04:31.880
is one of the most powerful learning

00:04:29.280 --> 00:04:33.680
mechanism we have. So, what's the action

00:04:31.880 --> 00:04:34.560
item here? You don't need a complex

00:04:33.680 --> 00:04:37.280
process. [music]

00:04:34.560 --> 00:04:39.680
Just close the source, look away. Say it

00:04:37.280 --> 00:04:40.343
back cold. Say it to the wall. I do it

00:04:39.680 --> 00:04:40.720
all the time.

00:04:40.343 --> 00:04:43.120
>> [music]

00:04:40.720 --> 00:04:45.840
>> If you can't, you don't own it yet. If

00:04:43.120 --> 00:04:48.000
you can, it's yours. So, testing is not

00:04:45.840 --> 00:04:49.600
just how you check your memory, it's how

00:04:48.000 --> 00:04:52.200
you build it. And now, let's go to the

00:04:49.600 --> 00:04:54.080
second step. Getting it right today

00:04:52.200 --> 00:04:57.520
means nothing if it's gone tomorrow.

00:04:54.080 --> 00:05:00.600
About a month ago, I got an email from

00:04:57.520 --> 00:05:02.840
Martin Schneider. He's an MIT grad and a

00:05:00.600 --> 00:05:06.240
founder CEO of a company called Remnote.

00:05:02.840 --> 00:05:08.960
And he had spent years thinking about

00:05:06.240 --> 00:05:11.720
one deceptively hard problem. How to

00:05:08.960 --> 00:05:14.040
help people remember what they learn.

00:05:11.720 --> 00:05:16.040
Now, when we got on the Zoom call, we

00:05:14.040 --> 00:05:18.280
ended up geeking out about this one

00:05:16.040 --> 00:05:20.320
question. Why do some people forget

00:05:18.280 --> 00:05:22.640
things so fast, even when they

00:05:20.320 --> 00:05:25.400
understood them yesterday? And he made

00:05:22.640 --> 00:05:28.200
one point that really stuck with me. The

00:05:25.400 --> 00:05:31.080
timing is the whole game. Review too

00:05:28.200 --> 00:05:33.800
soon, and the brain does not have to

00:05:31.080 --> 00:05:36.400
work hard. So, nothing durable gets

00:05:33.800 --> 00:05:38.840
built. Wait too long, and you're

00:05:36.400 --> 00:05:41.600
rebuilding from rubble. Almost nobody

00:05:38.840 --> 00:05:43.880
hits that window well just by instinct

00:05:41.600 --> 00:05:46.440
alone. This is why we cram before

00:05:43.880 --> 00:05:48.463
presentations and exams. It is the

00:05:46.440 --> 00:05:49.000
seductive trade-off, right? Testing

00:05:48.463 --> 00:05:50.920
[music]

00:05:49.000 --> 00:05:53.480
tells you whether you understood it.

00:05:50.920 --> 00:05:55.920
Retaining decides whether it survived

00:05:53.480 --> 00:05:58.080
the test of time. So, when Martin showed

00:05:55.920 --> 00:06:00.254
me what his team had built, I wanted to

00:05:58.080 --> 00:06:00.320
share here for two reasons.

00:06:00.254 --> 00:06:03.520
>> [music]

00:06:00.320 --> 00:06:06.080
>> First, this is an MIT startup focused on

00:06:03.520 --> 00:06:10.000
exactly the problem most of us never

00:06:06.080 --> 00:06:12.640
solve. Space repetition, active recall,

00:06:10.000 --> 00:06:13.280
AI-based learning. And second, I

00:06:12.640 --> 00:06:14.664
personally [music]

00:06:13.280 --> 00:06:15.600
became a fan of Martin and

00:06:14.664 --> 00:06:17.360
[clears throat] what his team was

00:06:15.600 --> 00:06:19.280
building. And that's why this video is

00:06:17.360 --> 00:06:22.200
sponsored by Remnote. Let's say I want

00:06:19.280 --> 00:06:25.080
to learn something everyone deals with,

00:06:22.200 --> 00:06:26.680
sleep. Specifically, why sleep

00:06:25.080 --> 00:06:29.120
deprivation destroys [clears throat]

00:06:26.680 --> 00:06:32.280
your ability to think clearly. So, I

00:06:29.120 --> 00:06:35.720
open Remnote and I start a note. I type

00:06:32.280 --> 00:06:39.000
the question, "What happens to the brain

00:06:35.720 --> 00:06:41.720
after 24 hours without [music] sleep?"

00:06:39.000 --> 00:06:43.560
Now, I hit the equal sign twice. Either

00:06:41.720 --> 00:06:46.080
you can write the answer or the AI

00:06:43.560 --> 00:06:48.720
suggest the answer. The important thing

00:06:46.080 --> 00:06:50.960
is that now it's a flashcard. Making a

00:06:48.720 --> 00:06:53.640
flashcard this way helps you clarify

00:06:50.960 --> 00:06:56.160
that concept in your head. Now, the real

00:06:53.640 --> 00:06:59.760
test. I close the source, I try to

00:06:56.160 --> 00:07:02.120
answer cold. Okay, I got this one wrong.

00:06:59.760 --> 00:07:05.200
Remnote shows me exactly where the gap

00:07:02.120 --> 00:07:07.120
is and corrects it immediately. This is

00:07:05.200 --> 00:07:10.120
where I learn. Now, what if I want to go

00:07:07.120 --> 00:07:13.280
deeper? I can upload a PDF and turn the

00:07:10.120 --> 00:07:16.120
whole thing into a cycle where I learn,

00:07:13.280 --> 00:07:18.600
test, learn, test. You can use AI to

00:07:16.120 --> 00:07:21.760
explain any line that you highlighted in

00:07:18.600 --> 00:07:24.320
the PDF. Or, you can make a card for you

00:07:21.760 --> 00:07:26.400
to remember it later. The idea is to

00:07:24.320 --> 00:07:28.160
teach yourself a little bit and test

00:07:26.400 --> 00:07:31.200
yourself on it. And you can grade

00:07:28.160 --> 00:07:33.440
yourself. The system tracks exactly

00:07:31.200 --> 00:07:35.230
which topics I have mastered and which

00:07:33.440 --> 00:07:36.040
one still need work. In the background,

00:07:35.230 --> 00:07:38.000
[music]

00:07:36.040 --> 00:07:40.520
Remnote is tracking this activity to

00:07:38.000 --> 00:07:42.960
decide when I should see this card

00:07:40.520 --> 00:07:45.600
again. This is very cool. The tool

00:07:42.960 --> 00:07:47.800
automatically schedules when you need to

00:07:45.600 --> 00:07:49.880
do your next practice on the specific

00:07:47.800 --> 00:07:52.160
card or concept. You don't have to

00:07:49.880 --> 00:07:54.480
remember to remember. The timing [music]

00:07:52.160 --> 00:07:56.190
is handled by the tool. This is how you

00:07:54.480 --> 00:07:56.200
fight that forgetting curve.

00:07:56.190 --> 00:07:58.120
>> [music]

00:07:56.200 --> 00:08:00.200
>> And now, what if I wanted to connect

00:07:58.120 --> 00:08:02.480
this to something I already know? I

00:08:00.200 --> 00:08:04.920
remember I had already made a flashcard

00:08:02.480 --> 00:08:08.480
on a concept called cortisol. [music]

00:08:04.920 --> 00:08:11.440
So, I type the at sign and search for

00:08:08.480 --> 00:08:14.560
cortisol. One keystroke and this [music]

00:08:11.440 --> 00:08:17.640
card is now wired to another note I have

00:08:14.560 --> 00:08:20.720
on stress response. It's not a lone fact

00:08:17.640 --> 00:08:23.320
anymore. It's part of a web. This is

00:08:20.720 --> 00:08:27.120
what Martin and his team built. What I

00:08:23.320 --> 00:08:29.760
like about this is that this tool and AI

00:08:27.120 --> 00:08:31.800
help you do the hard work of getting

00:08:29.760 --> 00:08:34.400
things into [music] your brain in a way

00:08:31.800 --> 00:08:36.719
that you understand them deeply. And it

00:08:34.400 --> 00:08:38.120
speeds your journey to mastery. The base

00:08:36.719 --> 00:08:39.919
app is free.

00:08:38.120 --> 00:08:42.400
There's a link below that you can use to

00:08:39.919 --> 00:08:43.800
try the pro version free for a month and

00:08:42.400 --> 00:08:45.920
see if you [music] like it. Now, are

00:08:43.800 --> 00:08:48.600
there other tools for learning fast and

00:08:45.920 --> 00:08:50.480
making it last? Sure. My team uses

00:08:48.600 --> 00:08:52.960
Notion to [music] store insights. They

00:08:50.480 --> 00:08:55.160
swear on it. I use Notebook LM to go

00:08:52.960 --> 00:08:57.320
deep. RemNote is where you go to

00:08:55.160 --> 00:08:59.480
actually remember them. The moment you

00:08:57.320 --> 00:09:00.320
learn something worth retaining,

00:08:59.480 --> 00:09:02.120
schedule [music]

00:09:00.320 --> 00:09:04.280
when you're going to come back to it.

00:09:02.120 --> 00:09:07.200
That's the best way to make sure you

00:09:04.280 --> 00:09:08.920
keep it. Memory is not a filing cabinet.

00:09:07.200 --> 00:09:11.440
It's a web. Research [music] published

00:09:08.920 --> 00:09:13.839
in Science shows that new learning

00:09:11.440 --> 00:09:16.040
becomes far more durable when it's

00:09:13.839 --> 00:09:18.440
connected to what you already know.

00:09:16.040 --> 00:09:20.920
Every connection you build is one more

00:09:18.440 --> 00:09:22.680
road back to that concept. And that's

00:09:20.920 --> 00:09:25.560
why when two people study the same

00:09:22.680 --> 00:09:27.720
material, one sounds very fluid and the

00:09:25.560 --> 00:09:30.400
other one freezes because one built a

00:09:27.720 --> 00:09:32.839
connected web. The other one built an

00:09:30.400 --> 00:09:34.600
isolated list of facts. This is one of

00:09:32.839 --> 00:09:37.320
the most trickiest challenges [music]

00:09:34.600 --> 00:09:40.400
that I see in our productivity culture

00:09:37.320 --> 00:09:43.680
today. So many of us spend more time

00:09:40.400 --> 00:09:47.240
designing and organizing our digital

00:09:43.680 --> 00:09:50.600
system, pages, folders, views, tags,

00:09:47.240 --> 00:09:53.480
databases, and so little time actually

00:09:50.600 --> 00:09:55.800
connecting ideas in our head. But a

00:09:53.480 --> 00:09:57.920
graveyard of ideas

00:09:55.800 --> 00:10:00.120
is still a graveyard. And this is why

00:09:57.920 --> 00:10:02.040
smart people still get exposed in

00:10:00.120 --> 00:10:04.080
meetings. Because if you cannot make

00:10:02.040 --> 00:10:06.440
these connections between insights in

00:10:04.080 --> 00:10:07.200
private, you cannot retrieve them in

00:10:06.440 --> 00:10:08.720
public. [music]

00:10:07.200 --> 00:10:10.640
Because when you're under pressure, you

00:10:08.720 --> 00:10:12.480
haven't built any path back to those

00:10:10.640 --> 00:10:14.360
insights. So every time you learn

00:10:12.480 --> 00:10:16.880
something worth keeping, ask one

00:10:14.360 --> 00:10:19.760
question. What does this remind me of?

00:10:16.880 --> 00:10:23.400
For instance, I once struggled with the

00:10:19.760 --> 00:10:26.400
idea of opportunity cost. So I connected

00:10:23.400 --> 00:10:29.040
it to a dinner menu. Every time I order

00:10:26.400 --> 00:10:30.800
a dish, I'm deciding not to eat

00:10:29.040 --> 00:10:33.720
everything else. Well, that's

00:10:30.800 --> 00:10:35.760
opportunity cost. After that, it never

00:10:33.720 --> 00:10:37.520
left. Chess grandmasters know this.

00:10:35.760 --> 00:10:41.480
Cognitive studies estimate they

00:10:37.520 --> 00:10:42.520
internalize between 50,000 and 100,000

00:10:41.480 --> 00:10:45.240
board patterns. [music]

00:10:42.520 --> 00:10:47.839
Now, they're not memorizing isolated

00:10:45.240 --> 00:10:49.839
positions. Instead, they're compressing

00:10:47.839 --> 00:10:52.040
thousands of experiences into [music]

00:10:49.839 --> 00:10:54.080
connected patterns. Now, they can draw

00:10:52.040 --> 00:10:56.040
from them instantly. So what's the

00:10:54.080 --> 00:10:58.080
action item? Every time you learn

00:10:56.040 --> 00:10:59.280
something worth keeping, ask one

00:10:58.080 --> 00:11:01.680
question.

00:10:59.280 --> 00:11:02.212
What does this connect to that I already

00:11:01.680 --> 00:11:02.440
know?

00:11:02.212 --> 00:11:05.800
>> [music]

00:11:02.440 --> 00:11:09.040
>> One link, one analogy, one related

00:11:05.800 --> 00:11:12.040
concept, that is how isolated facts

00:11:09.040 --> 00:11:15.680
become a usable network. The power is

00:11:12.040 --> 00:11:18.280
not in storage, it's in the linkage.

00:11:15.680 --> 00:11:21.720
What wires together is what fires

00:11:18.280 --> 00:11:24.000
together under pressure. In our AI era,

00:11:21.720 --> 00:11:27.160
this distinction will matter the most.

00:11:24.000 --> 00:11:30.000
There is a principle at MIT that shaped

00:11:27.160 --> 00:11:33.400
how I think about this. Every January,

00:11:30.000 --> 00:11:35.960
MIT pauses all formal classes for a

00:11:33.400 --> 00:11:38.238
month. It's called the IAP or

00:11:35.960 --> 00:11:38.480
independent activities period.

00:11:38.238 --> 00:11:40.080
>> [music]

00:11:38.480 --> 00:11:42.440
>> There's only one rule.

00:11:40.080 --> 00:11:45.520
Build something real. You can partner

00:11:42.440 --> 00:11:47.600
with any student from any department

00:11:45.520 --> 00:11:50.520
across the [music] campus and build

00:11:47.600 --> 00:11:52.840
whatever you want as long as it's

00:11:50.520 --> 00:11:55.280
related to what you've learned. And most

00:11:52.840 --> 00:11:57.440
of us feel like we don't know enough to

00:11:55.280 --> 00:11:59.240
build anything, but we all end up

00:11:57.440 --> 00:12:03.160
building something by the end of that

00:11:59.240 --> 00:12:06.560
month. In today's age with AI, fluency

00:12:03.160 --> 00:12:08.880
and intelligence are flowing free now.

00:12:06.560 --> 00:12:12.400
What is not free is the human

00:12:08.880 --> 00:12:16.040
experience, the judgment that comes from

00:12:12.400 --> 00:12:19.120
having built something, trying, failing,

00:12:16.040 --> 00:12:21.120
rebuilding. This is why this fourth move

00:12:19.120 --> 00:12:24.640
is so critical.

00:12:21.120 --> 00:12:28.080
You know, when I was in my 20s,

00:12:24.640 --> 00:12:30.040
I felt completely unformed. [music]

00:12:28.080 --> 00:12:33.120
I could not focus.

00:12:30.040 --> 00:12:34.568
I could not learn. I failed every test

00:12:33.120 --> 00:12:34.600
that I took.

00:12:34.568 --> 00:12:37.120
>> [music]

00:12:34.600 --> 00:12:39.640
>> I could not retain what I learned. I did

00:12:37.120 --> 00:12:41.773
not know how to build a mind that could

00:12:39.640 --> 00:12:41.880
actually hold on to things.

00:12:41.773 --> 00:12:44.360
>> [music]

